Short:        GLQuake for R200 Radeons on 68k
Uploader:     marcus.gerards@googlemail.com (Marcus Gerards)
Author:       id Software, D. Boon, S. Haeuser, Cowcat, M. Gerards et al.
Type:         game/shoot
Version:      1.5
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.1
Distribution: Aminet
Requires:     R200 Radeon, driver/video/RadeonGL 0.14, 68040/68060
              with FPU, Quake data
Kurz:         GLQuake fuer R200-Radeons am 68k

GLQuake for R200 68k  --  RadeonGL edition 1.5  --  13.11.2026


WHAT IT IS

GLQuake for 68k Amigas with an R200 Radeon: the Radeon draws through
RadeonGL (driver/video/RadeonGL), the minigl.library for these cards on
Picasso96. It is Dennis Boon's and Steffen Haeuser's GLQuake for
minigl.library (game/shoot/MiniGL_Library_Quake1), built for the 68060
and the 68040, with crash fixes, picture fixes and a faster renderer --
and it uses RadeonGL's own extension to keep the world in the card's
memory and let the card cull and depth-test whole chunks of it.

It stays a plain minigl.library 29.1 program: on PiStorm3D, the classic
MiniGL or any other minigl.library 29.1 it runs too, with the classic
drawing instead of RadeonGL's extension.


WHAT IS NEW AGAINST THE ARCHIVE IT COMES FROM

Features:
  - QuakeLauncher, the start window: the game (Quake, the mission packs,
    a mod), screen size, 16 or 32 bits, vertical sync, sound, memory,
    more arguments; it starts the installed build and keeps the settings
  - Options > Video Options with three items: Choose screenmode (every
    screen mode of the library on a page of its own, switched live; a
    mode that does not fit says why), Display sync (off, ScrollVPort,
    vsync, ChangeScreenBuffer, or ChangeScreenBuffer without waiting for
    the DispMessage, marked unsafe; fastest first, ScrollVPort by
    default;
    switched at once with RadeonGL 0.12, at the next start with an older
    RadeonGL) and Screen depth (16 or 32 bit, from the next start);
    vid_sync 0-4 in the console
  - 32-bit screens (-bpp 32): smoother shading, no 16-bit dithering
  - Options > Sound Options (Paula or AHI, live), Mouse Look and Mouse
    Speed; menus and console scaled sharp at high resolutions
  - the Installer puts in only the build for the processor; the
    installed Quake drawer opens in a window of its own with its three
    icons in one row

Fixes:
  - the mouse port's left button works again after GLQuake (it set the
    port to auto-sensing; reported by midwan)
  - no more guru BF00 0002 and no frozen water on a 68060: the sound
    interrupt no longer uses the FPU
  - no more bright pixels at polygon seams (gl_keeptjunctions 1)
  - mouse look follows the mouse at any frame rate (no lost movement)
  - the mouse pointer stays hidden (with RadeonGL 0.7)
  - options menu: no empty last row, Lookstrafe reachable again
  - dynamic lights no longer shine through walls
  - two crashes in the QuakeC value printer (savegames) are gone
  - 68040: the underwater warp no longer uses libnix sin(); on
    FS-UAE's 68040 with OS 3.2's mathieeedoubtrans.library 47.2 it
    returned wrong values for large angles (not checked on a real
    68040)

Speed:
  - a 68060 build and a 68040 build, each without instructions its CPU
    would have to emulate
  - the world from a vertex array built at level load; with RadeonGL
    0.7 it stays in video memory and the card draws whole chunks
  - 8-bit lightmaps by default, faster world walk, cheaper models,
    lights, text and sound mixing
  - underwater surfaces with GLQuake's own sine instead of calls
    into mathieeedoubtrans.library: demo2 +17%
  - real play about 15% faster (exact caches for the server's map
    queries, a compact map tree)

SPEED

GLQuake's timedemo (all frames, no sound) on an A4000D: 68060/50
(CyberStorm MK II), Mediator, Radeon 9200, AmigaOS 3.2, Picasso96 3.6,
RadeonGL 0.14 with its command check off, two buffers without vertical
sync. Frames a second, each 1.5 figure the mean of three runs:

    Demo   Screen       before     1.5
    demo1  320 x 240      24.4    32.5   (+33%)
    demo1  640 x 480      23.7    31.8   (+34%)
    demo1  1280 x 720     23.3    32.0   (+37%)
    demo2  320 x 240      21.7    29.1   (+34%)
    demo2  1280 x 720     17.9    28.5   (+59%)
    demo3  320 x 240      21.4    28.0   (+31%)
    demo3  1280 x 720     19.9    26.9   (+35%)

"Before": the Aminet source already built for the 68060 and with the
sound interrupt fixed, on RadeonGL 0.6, measured on another day (the
machine varies by a few per cent from one session to the next). Real play
(with the server, which a timedemo skips) gained about 15% more. The
68060 sets the pace, not the Radeon.

Display sync, demo1, the same session:

    Display sync               1280 x 720   1920 x 1080 (50 Hz)
    off (tears)                   32.0          28.6
    ScrollVPort (the default)     31.9          30.8
    vsync                         29.8          22.7


INSTALLATION

Install RadeonGL 0.14 first. Then double-click Install in this
package: it asks for the drawer that holds Quake's data drawer (id1) and
copies the build for your processor (GLQuake for a 68060, GLQuake040 for
a 68040), the start window QuakeLauncher, the start script PlayQuake and
the ReadMe there; a GLQuake already there that is not this edition is
kept as GLQuake.old. Double-click QuakeLauncher to play. ReadMe has the
details and the new options.


SOURCE CODE

The complete source is in the archive's Source drawer.


MADE WITH AI

This edition was made with the help of AI. Claude, an AI model by
Anthropic, wrote most of the changes, the tests and the documentation,
working under the direction of Marcus Gerards, who made the design
decisions. Every change was measured and tested on real hardware.


